So before my change:
```
(finite, False) -> {('zero', False)}
(finite, True) -> {('infinite', False)}
```

After my change (with `even -> finite` and `odd -> finite`):
```
(finite, False) -> {('odd', False), ('zero', False), ('even', False)}
(finite, True) -> {('infinite', False)}
```

So my change adds `(finite, False) -> ('odd', False)` and `(finite, False) -> ('even', False)`. This is because `even -> finite` means if `even` is `True`, then `finite` is `True`. The contrapositive is: if `finite` is `False`, then `even` is `False`.

So `oo.is_even` becomes `False` (from `None` before) and `oo.is_odd` becomes `False` (from `None` before).

